WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:12.600
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to Lecture 6 of CME 296.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.240
So today is a very
exciting day because we'll

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.960
be talking about how to
train a model in practice,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.640
a text-to-image
generation model.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.200
And as you know, the
first half of this class

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.840
was all about preparing the
foundational theoretical

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.600
foundations for us to
be able to do that.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.160
And in this second part,
we'll be really talking about

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.800
more the practical aspects,
and today will be no exception.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.520
So as usual, before
we start, we're

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.520
going to just recap
what we saw last time.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.920
So if you remember
last lecture, we

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.230
saw the main image
generation architectures.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.030
And we started with
the big one, the UNet,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.110
which was trendy up until 2022.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.070
And we saw that
among the things we

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.110
wanted to see in an
image generation model,

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.870
we wanted the model that
was able to understand

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.830
the global structure
of an image,

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.990
also capture the local
details, able to receive

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.310
external signals, for
instance, a condition,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.950
and be computationally
tractable.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:38.430
And we saw that the UNet was
relying on an inductive bias.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.830
Which was something that was
reflected by the convolution

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:49.110
operation, which is mimicking
how humans look at pictures.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:54.350
And we saw that the unit was
composed of two main phases.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.770
The first one is the
downsampling phases,

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:03.810
where we put an input, a
noisy latent, that we then

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.050
perform convolution operations
and downsampling operations

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.290
so that we can get a
bigger receptive field that

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.450
would enable us to have
a global understanding

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.570
of the input in the bottleneck.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.830
So that was the first phase.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.090
And then the second phase
with the upsampling phase,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.290
which was composed of
upsampling operations,

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.650
for instance, the transpose
convolution, where

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:37.130
we were able to get back to the
dimension of interest, which

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.850
is the same dimension
as the input.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:45.210
And we saw that there are these
copy-and-crop connections that

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:50.290
were allowing us to transport
the local details that we were

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:55.210
able to compute in the
downsampling phase and couple

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.890
that with the global
understanding that we had,

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.640
the global structure
understanding that we

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.240
had from the bottleneck.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.960
So that was the UNet.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.240
We saw that the UNet
was great, but the UNet

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.440
had a failure case,
which was that there

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.080
was no direct connection
between different local parts

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.480
of the image.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.080
And for an example, such as
someone looking at oneself

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.480
in the mirror.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.960
You need to be aware
of the local details

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.720
from two different
parts of the image.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.280
So this was motivating us to
look at another architecture,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.320
which is the diffusion
transformer, which was published

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.760
in 2022, late 2022, which
relies on the concept

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:53.990
of self-attention, where each
patch of the input latents

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.070
attends to one another, allowing
these direct connections

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.910
to form.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:06.390
And as a result, we were able
to have this concern mitigated.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:10.750
And we also saw a way
to inject conditions

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.010
into the architecture.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:18.589
So we spend some time on the
adaptive layer normalization,

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.990
which was taking the
condition and the time step

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:27.830
as inputs that was modulating
the token embeddings

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.950
via this gates, shift,
and scale factors.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.570
And we also saw that there were
very many variants, actually.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.290
It was not just the
diffusion transformer,

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.450
there were many variants of
the diffusion transformer,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.390
such as the multi-modal
diffusion transformer, which

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:55.850
is now more trendy, which was
looking at making the input

00:04:55.850 --> 00:05:02.510
text as a standalone modality
that was being injected as input

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:07.330
as opposed to an afterthought
via the modulation,

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:12.250
mainly because the adaptive
layer norm was injecting

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:18.650
the condition across all patch
embeddings in a uniform way.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:23.330
And we didn't want to have
that, so we also saw the MMD.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.930
And of course,
these architectures

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:27.830
are not the only ones
that are out there.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.930
There are many variations.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:34.250
So hopefully, that
lecture was a lecture

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:39.050
that gave you the intuitive
tools to at least understand why

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:40.550
certain components are used.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:44.570
All good on this?

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.570
So this was last lecture.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:50.840
We also saw the timeline,
which I briefly mentioned here.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.660
So UNet largely dominated
the early 2020s,

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.680
if I can say it like this.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:04.120
And then the DIT-based models
are dominating the architectures

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:12.200
that are coming out nowadays,
from end of 2022 onwards.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.600
And what are we
going to do today?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:20.580
Today, we're going to focus
on practical applications,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.640
on how we can actually train a
text to image generation model.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.040
And I know so far we have
seen different components

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.880
of that overall system.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.160
We've seen the
actual backbone that

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:43.720
is allowing us to denoise
the noise latents,

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.920
which is for instance
here a DIT-based model.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.820
We've seen that there is
also another model involved,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:55.420
the VAE, which is allowing us to
have a latent space in which we

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.940
can operate, which lowers
the computational burden

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.780
of our training process
and generation process.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:07.620
And we've also seen that there
are multiple models we could

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.100
use that could embed
conditions that we would then

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.180
have as input.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.980
So to be clear,
today we're going

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:22.940
to focus on how to train the
image generation model itself,

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.260
and we're not going to focus
on the VAE and the embedding

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.860
models.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:33.100
So just for reference,
we will actually

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:39.860
dig into embedding models next
week with the multi-modal LLMs.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:42.400
But I'm going to just
push it for next week.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.660
More on that soon.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:46.950
Cool.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.770
So let's start.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:55.290
And I just first want us to
look at this whole problem

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.130
at a very high level.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:01.650
And in particular, look
at the overall process

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:07.170
that allows us to go
from an initialized model

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:11.770
to a model that is able
to work in production.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:16.890
So the first step, we have
our image generation model.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:23.530
We first teach it how to
generate images, any images.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:27.810
This phase is typically
called the pre-training phase.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.130
And it's quite
compute intensive.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:37.210
It involves a lot of data and
a lot of work on the data side.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:39.330
So that's number
one, and it's a phase

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:43.250
that all models go through.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:48.640
Then we have a second stage
that is called the post-training

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.720
stage, where we take that
pre-trained model that already

00:08:53.720 --> 00:09:00.080
knows how to generate images,
and we teach it how to generate

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.080
"good" images.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.380
And we will see what good means.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:12.760
We could also optionally
also personalize

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.640
our model to generate
specific kinds of images.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.420
And we will see this
optional third step,

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:23.400
which is here called tuning.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.980
And then at the end
of this lecture,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:29.120
we'll also look at
how we can actually

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.720
make our model be able
to work in production

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:34.440
in an efficient way.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.160
And here we will look
at distillation models

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.840
or distillation
methods, I should

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:48.780
say, that allow our model to
generate samples in fewer steps,

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:52.000
because we've seen that usually
these image generation models,

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.260
they're working in
an iterative fashion.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:01.900
So you always have to have
one the initial noise as input

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.940
to get the let's
say vector field,

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.880
and then iterate on that
many times potentially.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.940
And here we want to
shorten the amount of times

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:19.100
we need to do that so that we
can make this process just less

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.860
costly and reduce the latency,
which is typically what we care

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:28.580
about for production use cases.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:33.220
Everyone good with
the big, big steps?

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:36.420
So we'll start with the first.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:40.860
Even before starting, actually,
we'll be talking about the loss

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.650
that we will use for
our training process.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.370
And I think you
already what the loss

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:51.290
is because we've spent the
first three lectures talking

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:54.410
about three different
perspectives on getting

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.890
to that stage.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:01.530
So here, this part
is mainly around just

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:07.370
all agreeing on what
those losses are and see

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.570
what are the changes that happen
in practice to these losses

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:15.230
to make the training
process more optimized.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:18.290
And you will see what I mean.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:24.130
So first, I'm going to recap
what we saw during the three

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:26.050
first lectures.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.970
So the first one, if you
recall, was diffusion with DDPM

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:38.530
where our thought process was
we take clean images that we

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:44.320
corrupt step by step,
so you have this xt that

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.200
is a function of the clean image
and the noise that you add.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.240
And we saw that we could
derive a tractable loss,

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.920
and you end up
with a formula that

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.480
is allowing you to denoise
the noise with the formula

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:06.480
on the slides by
parameterizing a model that

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:12.400
is geared towards predicting the
noise to remove epsilon theta.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.020
And here, the loss
is as follows.

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:19.200
So it's the expectation
of just the L2 distance

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.560
between your predicted
noise to remove

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.520
and the actual noise
that was added,

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:33.120
and it's an expectation
over the time steps,

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.440
the clean images that are
part of your training sets,

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.340
and then the noise that
is typically sampled

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.540
from a Gaussian distribution.

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.300
Then in Lecture 2, we had
this second perspective

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.740
of looking at the same problem,
which was, OK, in order

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.500
to recover clean
images, let's actually

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:59.980
look at where the
clean images are

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:05.220
by estimating the score, which
is acting like a compass.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:09.340
And here we saw that there
was a stochastic differential

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:14.060
equation, way of putting
this, which was given by dx

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:19.700
is equal to some drift term
and some diffusion term,

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:25.740
along with the continuous noise,
which was the Wiener process.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.320
So this was the forward process.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.980
And then, the reverse
process was something

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:34.940
that was popping up the score.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.250
And this just
highlighted the fact

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:40.410
that we needed to estimate
the score in order

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:45.410
to denoise into clean images.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:53.730
And here, the last was an
expectation over the score

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:57.970
that you wanted to estimate
minus the actual score

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:02.050
of the noise distribution.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:04.810
So that's the second
loss that we saw.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.650
And then the third perspective,
which was lecture 3,

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.570
was the flow
perspective, where we

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:17.010
said that in order to go
from noise to our target

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:21.010
distribution, we're going
to interpret that problem

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.010
as a transport problem.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:27.810
And here, we focused
on the velocity

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.330
or also known as
the vector field,

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:38.520
that could be used using an ODE
solver like the Euler method.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:44.840
And we had a loss that was
regressing the vector field

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:48.620
by just taking two points.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:51.320
So one from the target
distribution, x1,

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:55.080
and then x0 from the noisy
distribution, typically

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.760
a Gaussian distribution.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:02.880
And we ended up with
this last function.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.000
Now, one thing I want
to say, and maybe

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:10.720
I've said it some
time in the past,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:16.880
nowadays, people mainly focus on
the flow-matching perspective.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:21.720
So I just want us to all be
aligned that moving forward,

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.680
by default, we'll be
using the flow matching

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.940
loss as the default loss.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.460
Does that sound good?

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:35.860
So let's look at this loss.

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:39.540
So here what we're
doing is we're

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:43.460
optimizing on the
parameter theta

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:48.020
such that we minimize
the loss on the slides

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:52.660
in particular by taking
the expectation over time

00:15:52.660 --> 00:16:00.140
steps over data points in
the training data, so pdata,

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:04.020
and over noisy observations
that are part of a Gaussian

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.500
distribution.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:10.020
And in particular,
we are taking--

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.900
so we were saying we
were taking t drawn

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:18.740
from a uniform distribution.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.820
And I just want us to
pause on this statement

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:26.140
and see if this
actually makes sense.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:31.210
But first, just as a
reminder, here by t we

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:40.090
level where t equals 0
corresponds to a lot of noise,

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:45.090
t equal to 1 means
no noise at all,

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:51.010
and t is somewhere in between
means a little bit noisy.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:56.010
So I'm going to ask
you some questions.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:17:03.650
So let's suppose we want to
figure out from an input that

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:07.530
is extremely noisy,
think of it as x0,

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:13.369
whether making a prediction
on where to go from x0

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:17.250
is an easy or a hard task.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:20.109
And so I'm just going
to make our life easier.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:23.490
So if you remember
the illustration

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.300
we had-- so we have the
noisy distribution that

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:28.050
is the one on the left
and then the target

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:29.150
distribution on the right.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:35.150
So what we're saying is if
we sample a noisy latent that

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:40.070
is very noisy, is the
task of predicting

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:43.410
where to go an easy
task or a hard task?

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.790
Yeah, easy.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:52.390
Do you want to say easy?

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:55.030
Yes, so it's indeed easy.

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:56.590
Now, why is it easy?

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:02.870
So at the very beginning,
the input that you have

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.490
is actually void
of all information.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:10.710
You don't anything about
where you're going.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.870
So what your model will
be incentivized to do

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:18.590
will be to just try
to predict if you

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:23.190
want the average direction,
where you need to go.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.770
So regardless of how much
time you spend learning that,

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:28.680
it will just learn
roughly where to go,

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:31.540
which will be if you
want the mean of where

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:37.740
all the observations in your
target data distribution are.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:42.660
So no matter how much time you
spend in that region of noise,

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.020
you will be incentivized to
predict the mean of where

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.580
all the observations are.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.620
So that's why I say
it's an easy task.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:53.640
So now, how about
if t is close to 1,

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:59.020
meaning that we're already very,
very advanced in the denoising

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:02.480
process and there is
almost no noise left?

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.960
Is it an easy task or a hard
task to where to go from there?

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:13.740
Hard?

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:17.020
Yeah, OK, so we have one heart.

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.900
Yep.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:20.580
Easy again.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.940
OK, so let's see.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:25.130
So let's try to think.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:29.130
So when t is close
to 1, what that means

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:36.250
is that the input xt is
already almost clean.

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:38.730
So we're almost there.

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:41.850
So what that means
is you only need

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:45.570
to make a few tweaks
to little details

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:49.010
to have a fully denoised image.

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:54.090
And in that sense, it
is actually an easy task

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:59.010
because you already have an
image that is almost clean

00:19:59.010 --> 00:19:59.590
and so on.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:05.850
The illustration, it corresponds
to if your observation is

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:09.470
already part of the
target data distribution,

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:14.010
and you just need to do a
small step towards having

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:16.810
your final observation.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.410
OK, so let's do the last one.

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:24.990
How about if your input image is
not super, super, super noisy,

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:31.190
it's not almost clean, it's
somewhere in the middle?

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:32.810
Is the task easy?

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.410
Is the task of knowing
where to go easy or hard?

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.790
Yep.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:41.330
Hard?

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:42.670
And same here?

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:45.870
OK, so yeah, you're
completely right.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:49.150
And to illustrate the
point that you're saying,

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:53.790
we are indeed in
this space at a point

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:58.230
where we need to figure
out about where to go.

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:02.150
But even thinking about it
from an intuitive perspective,

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:06.390
let's assume that your target
distribution is composed

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:09.310
of images of teddy bears.

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.590
If you're in the
middle, then it means

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:16.230
that roughly what the body is
like, what the head is like,

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.050
you know a little bit
where the arms like,

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:19.990
but there's a lot of things
that you need to figure out

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:20.865
about where to place.

00:21:20.865 --> 00:21:23.100
You need to figure out
what kind of texture

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.100
you want your teddy
bear to develop.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.440
You need to put the eyes.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.060
You need to put a
bunch of things.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.860
And these are all hard
decisions that you

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:37.940
need to take at time steps that
are typically in the middle.

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:42.340
And for that reason,
t equal to 0.5

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:46.580
is a hard, hard
task for the model.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:52.180
So if you remember,
what we saw was

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:57.220
that we were drawing our time
step when computing the loss

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:00.020
from a uniform distribution.

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:05.340
So what that means is
we're asking our model

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:11.260
to try to be good at predicting
the target as many times

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:16.940
for easy tasks as much
as for hard tasks.

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:21.890
And this is the reason why,
in practice, people actually

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:27.050
do not sample the time step
from a uniform distribution.

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:30.750
They actually sample from
another distribution,

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:35.130
which we're going to see in
a second, that emphasizes

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:38.410
on middle steps more
than it emphasizes

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:43.730
on either the early or
the late, late steps.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:47.750
And the distribution that
people in general use

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:52.410
typically is called the
logit-normal distribution.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.470
So the formula on the
slide may look scary,

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:58.250
but it's actually quite simple.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:00.810
So what we're saying is--

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:04.770
so first of all, I'm going
to define what logit means.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:09.810
So the logit of a
quantity-- so let's suppose

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:12.370
the logit of our time step t.

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:21.390
--is defined as the log of
t over 1 minus t as follows.

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:26.270
And so t is between 0 and 1.

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:31.230
So if t is between 0
and 1, then logit of t

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:36.910
is anywhere between minus
infinity to plus infinity.

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:42.910
And what we're saying
is if we sample

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:52.950
x from a normal distribution,
which is between minus infinity

00:23:52.950 --> 00:24:03.590
to plus infinity, then if we
express t as a function of x,

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:04.830
which--

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:09.230
I'm looking at the time,
we will probably not do.

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:16.310
You will have something like
this, which is just a sigmoid.

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:22.060
And that quantity will
have a distribution

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:26.340
that will be really
centered in the middle.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:32.020
So you may ask me, "Well, why do
you not sample directly t from

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.740
a normal distribution?"

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:36.120
Maybe it's a question you ask.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:39.780
But the reason why you don't do
that is the normal distribution

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.660
is a distribution that
can take any values,

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:47.060
and you only want
values between 0 and 1.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:51.380
So that mathematical trick
is just a way for you

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:55.880
to guarantee that
in between 0 and 1

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:59.880
because here the sigmoid
is between 0 and 1.

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:06.420
Does that make sense?

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:07.200
OK, great.

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:10.060
I just want this formula
or this distribution

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:14.800
to not seem like it's
coming out of nowhere.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:18.280
So the good thing
with this distribution

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:24.600
is you can tweak the way
the distribution is shaped.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.060
So you can play with the mean
and the standard deviation,

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:31.780
so you can shift it towards the
time steps that you care about.

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:33.680
So for instance, if
you in your use case

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:39.400
care about the later
steps, you can do that.

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:44.400
And so that is the reason why
people use that distribution.

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.120
But I'm not done
with time steps.

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:51.520
There's another thing I
want to talk to you about.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:54.960
So we're talking
about noise levels,

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:59.040
but what we actually
care about is how much

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.740
noise is perceived by yourself.

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:09.200
So if you take the same
image, one in low resolution

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:12.640
and the other one
in high resolution--

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:19.410
and if you apply the same amount
of noise, which, from the slide,

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:22.190
I hope you can see, that the one
on the left, the low resolution

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:27.750
one, feels more noisy
than the one on the right.

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.650
So same image,
different resolution,

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:32.310
but same noise level.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:36.270
The one on the left feels more
noisy than the one on the right.

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:39.230
So something tells
you that you also

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:43.670
need to take that into
account in some way.

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:48.070
So right now, we're not taking
that into account, but not

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:49.430
for long.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.710
So you will see that
in papers, there

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:55.630
is this concept of
time step shifting

00:26:55.630 --> 00:27:00.990
as a function of resolution,
where the idea is to rescale

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:07.670
the noise level in a way that
reflects the perceived noise

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:10.350
that you see.

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:14.820
And so I just want to pause
on this concept for a second.

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:20.020
Why would you perceive a
low-resolution image would

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:23.438
be more noisy than a
high-resolution one

00:27:23.438 --> 00:27:24.480
for the same noise level?

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:25.840
Why would you have to do that?

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:28.540
Or why would you think that?

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:31.940
Well, there is one
thing with images,

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:37.420
which is that they have a
lot of spatial correlation.

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.500
Meaning for a given
pixel, you have--

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:44.940
you are very likely to have
pixels around it that have

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:49.060
the same value or same color.

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:52.820
And so remember that
when you add your noise,

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:57.580
you're adding Gaussian
noise with mean 0.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:01.700
So what that means is if you
have very few pixels, when

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:06.660
you're in low resolution,
then if the pixel that

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:08.620
is responsible for,
let's say, the color

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:12.520
that characterizes how good
your image looks, let's

00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:15.320
say, at a particular angle--

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:20.780
if that one gets noised, then
you're losing that information.

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:23.600
You're not able
to see it anymore.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:26.920
But in the
high-resolution case, you

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:31.760
have multiple pixels
surrounding that region.

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:33.920
So even if one gets
noise, the other one

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:36.757
gets noise the other way because
remember that the mean should

00:28:36.757 --> 00:28:38.840
be equal to 0, the mean
of the noise, added noise,

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:41.360
should be equal to
0, you will still

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:47.800
have an average value that
will reflect the underlying

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:54.800
pixels much more compared
to the low-resolution case.

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:58.020
So for that reason, I hope
the statement that I'm making,

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:01.560
which is, for a
given noise level,

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:05.640
things feel more noisy for
low-resolution images compared

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:07.020
to high-resolution ones--

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:09.677
I hope that one is intuitive.

00:29:09.677 --> 00:29:11.010
I'm going to pause for a second.

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:14.750
Does everyone agree
with my statement?

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:15.590
Yep?

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:22.270
OK, so there is, again, a very
scary formula on the slides.

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:25.710
The reason why there
is just to show you

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:30.350
how people shift time
steps in practice.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:34.790
So I would first want us to
look at the chart on the right.

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:40.750
So you see tn and tm.

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:46.910
So here, let's assume you have
two images, one of resolution n

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:54.470
and the other one of resolution
m, where n is smaller than m.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:30:01.750
So here for a
given tn, you would

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:09.300
want to have a higher tm because
for a low-resolution image,

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:13.340
for a given noise level,
you would want that noise

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:16.000
level to be higher for
the high-resolution image,

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:20.580
for the perceived
noise to be the same.

00:30:20.580 --> 00:30:24.460
So that is what this
formula is saying.

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:27.220
But I know it's not
intuitive, so what we'll do

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:33.940
is we'll take 10 minutes
to try to understand

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:35.240
what that is about.

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:45.620
So the formula on the
slide is all about

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:51.420
reflecting the change in
uncertainty of pixel values

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:54.940
that are noised under
different resolutions.

00:30:54.940 --> 00:31:00.380
So let's assume that
we have an image,

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:05.960
so you can think of
it as this, with--

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:08.480
OK, I'm being quick.

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:14.400
So you have, let's say,
height and some width.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.840
And let's say that n--

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:22.720
so let's say m is this one.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:28.480
And let's say you have a
smaller image with, let's say,

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:36.840
h, little h, and little w,
and n is equal to h times w.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:42.720
I'm just saying that n is
a lower resolution than m.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.760
That's just what I want to say.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:50.160
So here, what we
want to do is to try

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.800
to quantify the
uncertainty of what

00:31:54.800 --> 00:31:57.280
the underlying pixel values
are in the case where

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:01.520
we have noise for both cases.

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:09.510
So what I propose to do is to
just assume that these images--

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:14.330
let's assume that they all
have a certain value as pixels.

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:16.450
So pixels have three channels.

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:18.870
Let's assume here that
we have only one channel,

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:22.270
one value by component.

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:26.070
So here, let's assume that
each of these is, let's say,

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:28.770
equal to some value
c that is constant.

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:34.750
So far so good?

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:41.470
So let's assume that
for a given let's

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:48.550
say index i that tells you about
where you are in the image,

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:59.870
and for a given noise level
t, the value at that index

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:04.580
is something that is
given as a function of C,

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:10.620
the value of your pixel,
and the amount of noise

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:13.820
that you're adding to it.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:19.540
So here I just need to
put the noise level.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:23.580
So I told you in
lecture one that people

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:27.100
use different conventions
for time steps.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:30.380
And it just happens
that the formula here

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:35.020
and the paper here, they
used the reverse convention

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:36.660
when it comes to time steps.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:44.300
And in particular, they used
t equals 0, means clean, and t

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:49.980
equals 1, means noise,
which is the opposite

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:52.140
of what usually people do.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:56.620
But in order for us to
be consistent and end up

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:59.000
at the same formula,
we will just

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:03.960
assume that is the convention
that we're going to use.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:11.040
So here, what we're going
to say is it's 1 minus tc

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:14.253
plus t epsilon.

00:34:14.253 --> 00:34:15.420
Does everyone agree with me?

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:20.440
So this is the value of the
noise pixel at the noise level

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:22.800
t.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:30.020
So here epsilon is drawn
from a normal distribution.

00:34:33.199 --> 00:34:35.900
And so what can we
say about this one?

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:42.760
So we assume that this
quantity is a constant,

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:46.800
but we don't what that quantity
is when we look at an image.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:52.719
So our goal here is to
quantify the uncertainty

00:34:52.719 --> 00:34:56.880
if we were to estimate
c, which is a constant.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:35:00.190
So what I'm going to do
is to try to quantify

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:03.670
the uncertainty of c.

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:09.310
So here, just because
we have n elements,

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:14.150
so m here and n
here n elements, I'm

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:16.270
just going to leverage
the fact that we

00:35:16.270 --> 00:35:24.870
have this n elements to take the
average over all pixel values.

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.670
So I'm going to sum
over, let's say,

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:32.990
i my index, that's between
1 and the number of pixels,

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:34.910
of the zi(t).

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:42.590
And I'm going to call
this zt bar for average.

00:35:42.590 --> 00:35:45.370
So here, this thing is a
constant, so it doesn't move.

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:52.390
And this thing just gets--

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:59.570
so here, I'm going to write
this here plus the average.

00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:01.470
So I'm going to put the t here.

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:07.490
The sum of y from--
sorry, sorry.

00:36:07.490 --> 00:36:13.538
The sum of I from 1 to n of
the epsilons, which are--

00:36:13.538 --> 00:36:14.330
as a function of i.

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:18.570
Because the noise here
is different from one

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:21.650
pixel to another.

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:26.610
So here what I wrote is that
the average value of a pixel

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:33.130
is 1 minus t times
c plus t over n

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:36.370
of the sum of the noise values.

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:38.190
So you're going to
see why I did that.

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:56.390
So here, I just want
to remember our--

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:58.440
to remind you of our objective.

00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:05.400
Our objective is to quantify
the uncertainty in estimating c.

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:12.600
So I want us to first
see that zt bar is

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:19.880
equal to a constant plus a
sum of independent Gaussian

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:22.760
variables.

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:24.560
So what you can see
about-- what you

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:32.320
can say about this quantity
is that its mean is

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:36.320
1 minus t times c.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:38.920
And its variance-- let's see.

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:43.320
So t over n is a constant.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:49.160
So the variance of a constant
is just the constant squared.

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:53.430
And then the variance
of each epsilon i is 1,

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:57.070
and you're adding n of them.

00:37:57.070 --> 00:38:09.670
So it's n times 1, which
is t squared over n.

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:11.070
OK, great.

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:13.850
So why am I doing all of this?

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:17.710
I just want to remind all of us
that what we know-- what we want

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:20.470
is to estimate the
uncertainty of C.

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:23.310
So what is a good
estimator for C?

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:27.270
So C-- here, we
see that zt bar is

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:33.910
equal to minus C times C plus
something where the mean is 0.

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:46.950
So a good estimate of C could be
1 over 1 minus t times zt bar.

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:52.650
And what we want to quantify is
the uncertainty in estimating c.

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:58.890
So what is the uncertainty
around estimating c.

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:03.090
So this thing is a constant.

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:06.130
So the variance of
a constant is just--

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:09.130
so sorry, the
variance of a factor

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:13.090
in front of something else
is the constant squared

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:18.070
times the variance of this
one, which is t squared over n.

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:27.250
So what we're saying is
that the standard deviation

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:36.390
of our estimate is equal to the
square root of the variance,

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:45.916
which is t over 1 minus t
times 1 over square root of n.

00:39:45.916 --> 00:39:48.400
OK, so what does that mean?

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:53.440
That means that the
uncertainty that we have around

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:56.720
determining what the pixel
value, underlying pixel value,

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:05.640
is a function of 1 over the
square root of the resolution.

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:11.480
So as we increase
the resolution n,

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:15.120
that uncertainty gets smaller.

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:18.520
And so in order to get
that derivation, which

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:21.920
I don't think I will do,
it is just some math,

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:30.200
you would just equal
that uncertainty for n.

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:35.440
So it's t under
subscript n here.

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:37.100
And you do the same for m.

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:45.200
So you get exactly-- so the
noise level at time step--

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:53.630
for resolution m
will be as follows.

00:40:53.630 --> 00:41:00.270
And the goal that we have
is to determine the time

00:41:00.270 --> 00:41:06.150
step for resolution m such that
the uncertainty of determining

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:09.350
the pixel value is the same.

00:41:09.350 --> 00:41:16.910
So you just need to take
these two quantities

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:21.550
and just say that
they need to be equal,

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:25.610
and you find tm as
a function of tn.

00:41:29.870 --> 00:41:33.070
Does that make sense?

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:39.470
So the low-level derivation
is not that important.

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:41.210
What is important
is just the mindset,

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:46.650
which is that here we want to
shift the time step for higher

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:52.050
resolution, such that
we match this perceived

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:53.750
noise that we have.

00:41:57.330 --> 00:41:59.710
And so hopefully, when you
take a look at that formula,

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:03.470
you're not thinking, "OK,
where does that come from?"

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:06.750
OK, cool.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:08.970
So I think we're done
with the time steps.

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.670
Any questions on this?

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:13.170
Yep.

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:17.210
What is c again?

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:17.710
Yes.

00:42:17.710 --> 00:42:19.810
So the question is,
What is c again?

00:42:19.810 --> 00:42:23.450
So this was actually
a toy example

00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:27.410
that we had where
we said let's assume

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:32.010
that we have an image where all
the pixel values are constants,

00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:34.130
all equal to c.

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:38.850
So c is the value of the
pixel that is across all

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:41.330
our image in this toy example.

00:42:41.330 --> 00:42:45.420
And the only problem with
c-- we know it's constant,

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:50.440
but we don't what c is because
we have noised that image.

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:55.760
And this whole derivation
is about quantifying

00:42:55.760 --> 00:43:02.120
our uncertainty of what c is
if we were to estimate it.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:07.560
And what we said was that we
could quantify the uncertainty

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:11.000
through this standard deviation.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:14.040
And what we said was,
depending on the resolution

00:43:14.040 --> 00:43:18.740
at which you're operating, that
uncertainty is higher or lower.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:20.480
And in particular,
that uncertainty

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:24.040
is lower as the
resolution gets higher.

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:28.760
And this is all because you can
actually perform your estimation

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:31.520
by averaging across
all pixel values, which

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:37.360
are getting bigger and bigger
when the resolution increases.

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:38.720
Does that make sense?

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:40.240
OK, perfect.

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:41.350
Yeah, yep.

00:43:41.350 --> 00:43:44.910
So the question is, we said that
the diffusion process is taking

00:43:44.910 --> 00:43:47.890
place in the latent space, so m
and n are in the latent space?

00:43:47.890 --> 00:43:49.910
Yes.

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:52.630
Yep.

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:54.310
OK, perfect.

00:43:54.310 --> 00:43:56.510
Let's move on.

00:43:56.510 --> 00:43:58.310
We're done with the time steps.

00:43:58.310 --> 00:44:01.870
Now we're going to the
next training trick

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:04.190
that I wanted to
talk about, which

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:13.790
is helping our model with
learning representations faster.

00:44:13.790 --> 00:44:15.710
And how do we want to do that?

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:22.230
So here, the whole idea is to
leverage the representation

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:27.510
that some already pre-trained
encoders have learned

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:32.870
and incorporate that,
and incentivize our model

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:36.510
to match that representation.

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:42.330
So in other words, it's as if
you want to learn about a topic,

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:46.270
and I come and get you
a book about that topic.

00:44:46.270 --> 00:44:48.390
So you can learn
faster about the topic,

00:44:48.390 --> 00:44:51.210
so it's exactly
the same mindset.

00:44:51.210 --> 00:44:53.910
So here, REPA is the
name of the method.

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:57.810
It stands for
Representation Alignment.

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:03.370
And it consists of
coupling your loss

00:45:03.370 --> 00:45:07.970
with a loss that reflects how
similar the representations

00:45:07.970 --> 00:45:11.650
that you're obtaining within
your diffusion transformer

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:15.330
is with respect to what the
pre-trained encoder is giving

00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:18.090
you.

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:20.650
So that's what REPA is about.

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:22.890
So let's go step by step.

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:26.190
Here, we mentioned
the L loss for REPA,

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:28.170
but we have not seen
what that loss is.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:30.250
So we're going to see now.

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:36.090
So here what we're saying is
in the diffusion transformer,

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:39.160
you have several layers.

00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:43.280
So what you're saying is you're
taking the encoded output

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:45.440
of a given layer.

00:45:45.440 --> 00:45:48.280
So let's call that ht.

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:52.080
And here n, I believe,
represents the patch,

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:55.800
let's say, for a given patch n.

00:45:55.800 --> 00:46:00.560
And let's project
that on a space where

00:46:00.560 --> 00:46:07.440
we can compare the equivalent
clean patch representation

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:12.080
from the pretrained encoder,
and then have a similarity

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:15.680
operation between the two.

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:20.840
So in other words, we will
be penalizing our model

00:46:20.840 --> 00:46:25.980
for having a projection of the
encoded output of a given patch.

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:31.200
That is, noise we will penalize
our model from this being

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:34.320
dissimilar from
the representation

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:44.100
given by the pre-trained encoder
of that same patch, but cleaned.

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:46.240
So why do we do that?

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:49.355
So as I mentioned,
we're giving a book

00:46:49.355 --> 00:46:50.980
to someone who's
learning about a topic

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:52.480
so that they can learn faster.

00:46:52.480 --> 00:46:54.940
And that's exactly what happens.

00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:56.820
So what we're seeing
with this strategy

00:46:56.820 --> 00:47:01.860
is that it speeds up training
significantly, so here

00:47:01.860 --> 00:47:03.960
on the order of 18 times.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:07.460
So this graph
represents some measure

00:47:07.460 --> 00:47:11.220
of how good your images are
as a function of the training

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:12.660
iteration.

00:47:12.660 --> 00:47:16.100
And what the
authors did was just

00:47:16.100 --> 00:47:19.280
try out a bunch of different--

00:47:21.952 --> 00:47:23.160
they tried a bunch of things.

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:24.920
So they tried different models.

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:26.120
They tried different layers.

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:29.300
They tried-- so they did
a lot of ablation studies.

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:32.060
One of the things
that they saw was

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:39.730
if you were to perform that loss
on the embedding from earlier

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:44.370
layers, then you actually
get better results.

00:47:44.370 --> 00:47:47.730
And I just want to pause
on that statement for a bit

00:47:47.730 --> 00:47:50.770
just to understand
why that is the case.

00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:55.330
So our diffusion
transformer gets

00:47:55.330 --> 00:48:03.450
to compute a prediction
of where it wants to go.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:08.170
And in that sense,
the later layers

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:14.050
may actually involve
more local details

00:48:14.050 --> 00:48:17.370
rather than a semantic
representation

00:48:17.370 --> 00:48:19.330
of where you want to go.

00:48:19.330 --> 00:48:23.730
And so here that
pre-trained encoders,

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:25.850
some of them, what
they're good at

00:48:25.850 --> 00:48:30.970
is representing things in a
semantically meaningful way.

00:48:30.970 --> 00:48:34.680
And so one can
understand these facts

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:39.120
by saying that, OK, we actually
want the representation

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:41.800
from a layer that is supposed
to output something that

00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:44.520
is semantically
meaningful to align

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:50.160
with a semantic or semantically
meaningful representation.

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:55.360
I just wanted to vocalize
a possible interpretation.

00:48:55.360 --> 00:48:56.800
And then the last
thing I will say

00:48:56.800 --> 00:49:00.760
is that an interesting thing
is that the bigger your model,

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:07.200
the faster or the more benefits
you have from this method.

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:07.920
Cool.

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:11.660
And the REPA was
published in 2024,

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:14.440
but it is a technique
that I have personally

00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:20.440
seen in a lot of new papers,
so worth keeping in mind.

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:26.300
And that is concluding the
first preliminary parts.

00:49:26.300 --> 00:49:29.020
So now we're going
to first start

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:33.140
the first step of the lifecycle,
training lifecycle, which

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:35.900
is pre-training.

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:39.780
And so as a reminder,
in this first step,

00:49:39.780 --> 00:49:45.620
the goal is to teach our model
how to generate just images.

00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:50.260
And so here there
is a lot of work

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:53.180
that goes into
what kind of images

00:49:53.180 --> 00:49:57.140
you want your model
to learn from.

00:49:57.140 --> 00:50:00.120
And so I just have
this one slide,

00:50:00.120 --> 00:50:03.580
which is coming from-- so
it's an image from the Quinn

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:06.100
technical report,
which just tells you

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:08.820
that this is a very
time-consuming work

00:50:08.820 --> 00:50:14.740
that's involves data pipeline
with a bunch of filtering,

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:17.160
different resolutions,
cleaning, and so on.

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:19.840
So I just have one slide
to illustrate this,

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:21.720
but it's a very
time consuming step.

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:27.780
So just know that this is an
important part of the process.

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:30.190
There is a concept that I
want to talk to you about,

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:34.650
which is called
curriculum learning, which

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:42.090
is about teaching your model on
things in a progressive manner

00:50:42.090 --> 00:50:44.850
on the scale of difficulty.

00:50:44.850 --> 00:50:48.250
So in other words,
here, what we want

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:52.930
is to teach first our model
on doing the easy things

00:50:52.930 --> 00:50:56.370
before doing the hard things.

00:50:56.370 --> 00:50:59.090
And this is a practice
that is typically something

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:04.250
that people do as this just
helps the model learn better.

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:09.250
And so here, by "easy" I mean
low-resolution images that are

00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:12.650
of very easy aspect
ratio, so fixed square.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:14.170
You can think of that.

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:18.350
And then the input text
is simple and short,

00:51:18.350 --> 00:51:20.890
so for instance, a teddy bear.

00:51:20.890 --> 00:51:23.370
And then the hard
part is if you want

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:25.770
to predict something that
is of high resolution,

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:27.500
different aspect ratios.

00:51:27.500 --> 00:51:29.120
It's not a square anymore.

00:51:29.120 --> 00:51:33.640
And then, your prompt can be
much, much more complicated,

00:51:33.640 --> 00:51:36.720
such as the one on the slides.

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:41.560
And now you may ask me, well,
how would you handle images

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:43.640
of different resolutions?

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:48.840
And so I want us to remember
that the default architectures

00:51:48.840 --> 00:51:51.480
these days are DIT-based.

00:51:51.480 --> 00:51:58.520
And in DIT, what you do is you
divide your input into patches

00:51:58.520 --> 00:52:04.440
where each patch is
equivalent to some embedding.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:06.880
And so what happens
is for low resolution,

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:09.900
you will have a certain
number of patches,

00:52:09.900 --> 00:52:12.480
some number of tokens.

00:52:12.480 --> 00:52:17.680
And for high resolution, you
will just have more tokens.

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:18.460
Yes, exactly.

00:52:18.460 --> 00:52:20.260
So yeah, that's exactly that.

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:22.340
It's as if it was large context.

00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:26.540
And if you remember
the length t will

00:52:26.540 --> 00:52:30.080
be i, which is the
dimension of an input.

00:52:30.080 --> 00:52:33.460
So if it's a squared
over p squared.

00:52:33.460 --> 00:52:37.980
So that's the order of
magnitude to have in mind.

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:43.020
I will go ahead and move on to
the second step of the training

00:52:43.020 --> 00:52:47.700
lifecycle, which is
the post training step.

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:51.180
And so here what
we want to do is

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:56.780
to teach our model on how
to generate good images.

00:52:56.780 --> 00:53:02.140
Now you may tell me, "Well,
what does good mean?"

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:05.460
Well, you have, of
course several angles,

00:53:05.460 --> 00:53:10.340
but I'll talk about
two main names

00:53:10.340 --> 00:53:12.060
that you will see out there.

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:16.620
The first one is called
continued training, CT,

00:53:16.620 --> 00:53:22.460
which is around teaching your
model on a bunch of other things

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:26.863
that you may care about
in your task of interest.

00:53:26.863 --> 00:53:28.030
So I'll give you an example.

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:30.970
So let's suppose you have a
model that has been pre-trained

00:53:30.970 --> 00:53:36.750
on a bunch of nature
images, and cars, and so on,

00:53:36.750 --> 00:53:40.050
but what you want to do
is to have a model that

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:42.890
generates images of teddy bear.

00:53:42.890 --> 00:53:45.490
Well, what you'll
do here is you will

00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:49.230
have a dataset of
what you care about,

00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:51.450
and here that is teddy bears.

00:53:51.450 --> 00:53:55.810
And you will just continue
your training on that data set.

00:53:55.810 --> 00:53:57.330
So that part, you
can think of it

00:53:57.330 --> 00:54:03.010
as focusing on increasing
your knowledge.

00:54:03.010 --> 00:54:06.770
Then you have another category
of post-training methods, which

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:10.290
are called supervised
fine-tuning.

00:54:10.290 --> 00:54:14.170
Same thing, but
here the mindset is

00:54:14.170 --> 00:54:17.930
to focus on the behavior
of your model as opposed

00:54:17.930 --> 00:54:20.650
to increasing the knowledge.

00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:26.520
So in that case, what that means
is generating an image that

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:30.600
is more aesthetically pleasing,
that has better lighting,

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:35.120
or that just follows
the text better.

00:54:35.120 --> 00:54:40.280
So these are the kinds of
dimensions that you care about.

00:54:40.280 --> 00:54:45.440
And up until now,
we've only seen

00:54:45.440 --> 00:54:50.400
how to teach our
model on what to do,

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:55.640
but we have not taught our
model on what not to do.

00:54:55.640 --> 00:55:00.920
And that is the objective of
the second category of methods,

00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:05.960
which is preference tuning
methods, where for a given

00:55:05.960 --> 00:55:10.640
prompt, you may have two images
where one of those images

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:14.760
is something that you want to
happen more than another one.

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:18.360
So here I will illustrate an
example of the left image, which

00:55:18.360 --> 00:55:21.220
is an image of a teddy
bear reading, very high

00:55:21.220 --> 00:55:24.940
resolution, good lighting,
and aesthetically pleasing,

00:55:24.940 --> 00:55:29.180
and the one on the right,
which is not as aesthetically

00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:30.340
pleasing.

00:55:30.340 --> 00:55:34.420
And what you want to do is
to incentivize your model

00:55:34.420 --> 00:55:37.540
to generate an
image that is more

00:55:37.540 --> 00:55:41.380
aligned with human preferences.

00:55:41.380 --> 00:55:45.500
So here the thumbs
up and thumbs down

00:55:45.500 --> 00:55:50.660
are typically either human
ratings or some measure

00:55:50.660 --> 00:55:53.180
that you can obtain
with some model

00:55:53.180 --> 00:55:56.060
that we will talk
about next lecture.

00:55:56.060 --> 00:56:01.780
But you will need to have some
measure of pairwise comparison

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:04.700
or listwise comparison
of different images.

00:56:04.700 --> 00:56:10.100
And in this case, the left image
being better than the right one.

00:56:10.100 --> 00:56:13.260
So how do you do that?

00:56:13.260 --> 00:56:16.300
So before we move
further, I just

00:56:16.300 --> 00:56:21.050
want to say that the field
is still a developing field,

00:56:21.050 --> 00:56:27.530
and that next to it, there's
also the text field, the LLM

00:56:27.530 --> 00:56:30.050
field, that has been growing.

00:56:30.050 --> 00:56:32.290
And so one observation
is that you

00:56:32.290 --> 00:56:35.290
will find the same
kinds of techniques

00:56:35.290 --> 00:56:38.970
that are used in
the LLM space be

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:43.090
adapted for the text-to-image
generation space.

00:56:43.090 --> 00:56:45.090
And that is something
that I'm going to show you

00:56:45.090 --> 00:56:49.770
in the slides that will follow.

00:56:49.770 --> 00:56:55.410
So the first method that
is around tuning your model

00:56:55.410 --> 00:57:01.690
to incentivize generating
images that are more

00:57:01.690 --> 00:57:04.930
aligned with human
preferences is a method called

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:08.690
reward feedback learning.

00:57:08.690 --> 00:57:12.410
So I'll go step by step
into what that does.

00:57:12.410 --> 00:57:16.870
So as I mentioned, what you
do is you take a prompt,

00:57:16.870 --> 00:57:21.390
and then you have a bunch of
images related to that prompt.

00:57:21.390 --> 00:57:25.790
And what you do is you try to
get some sense of which one

00:57:25.790 --> 00:57:29.470
is better than the other.

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:32.510
So you can either
get pairwise ratings

00:57:32.510 --> 00:57:34.910
or even list wise ratings.

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:39.830
So for instance, A is better
than B, than C, et cetera.

00:57:39.830 --> 00:57:46.150
And from there, the idea
is to train a reward model

00:57:46.150 --> 00:57:51.350
that takes in a
prompt and an image,

00:57:51.350 --> 00:57:54.630
and that gets you a
score that tells you

00:57:54.630 --> 00:57:57.790
how good that image was
with respect to the score,

00:57:57.790 --> 00:58:02.830
or how good that image was
with respect to the prompt.

00:58:02.830 --> 00:58:05.790
And so here we will not
go through the math.

00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:07.790
So in case you're
interested, we actually

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:13.510
have a whole lecture on that
in our sister class, CME 295.

00:58:13.510 --> 00:58:19.620
But long story short is that you
can use a pairwise loss based

00:58:19.620 --> 00:58:23.920
on formulation called the
Bradley-Terry formulation

00:58:23.920 --> 00:58:28.340
and train a model that
does that for you.

00:58:28.340 --> 00:58:32.780
So at the end of this step,
you get a reward model

00:58:32.780 --> 00:58:35.300
that takes in a prompt,
that takes in an image,

00:58:35.300 --> 00:58:40.420
and that tells you how good
that image was to this prompt.

00:58:40.420 --> 00:58:45.820
So what you care
about is to tune

00:58:45.820 --> 00:58:49.260
your text-to-image
generation model

00:58:49.260 --> 00:58:53.500
to produce images
that are higher

00:58:53.500 --> 00:58:58.020
rewards, because the reward
model that you trained

00:58:58.020 --> 00:59:03.380
is something that hopefully
aligns with human preferences.

00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:06.440
So if the reward model
says that one is good,

00:59:06.440 --> 00:59:09.340
then hopefully, it's
something that is aligned

00:59:09.340 --> 00:59:11.420
with what the human would say.

00:59:11.420 --> 00:59:17.290
And what you want is to
train your image generation

00:59:17.290 --> 00:59:25.450
model in order to produce
images that are higher rewards.

00:59:25.450 --> 00:59:28.150
So there is some math involved,
but at a very high level,

00:59:28.150 --> 00:59:32.610
what we do is we take
the noisy latent,

00:59:32.610 --> 00:59:38.370
we predict or generate an image
for which we have the reward.

00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:41.950
But in this case, the good
thing is that we have the model.

00:59:41.950 --> 00:59:43.110
We have the reward model.

00:59:43.110 --> 00:59:46.650
It is a reward model that
we have at our disposal.

00:59:46.650 --> 00:59:52.250
So it is actually something
that is differentiable.

00:59:52.250 --> 00:59:57.290
And so here, you
can back propagate

00:59:57.290 --> 00:59:59.470
the loss or the
objective that you have,

00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:03.090
which is to maximize those
rewards back into your image

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:07.290
generation model in order
to incentivize that model

01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:13.670
to produce images that
are higher rewards.

01:00:13.670 --> 01:00:17.990
So that is reward
feedback learning.

01:00:17.990 --> 01:00:21.110
The second method
I will introduce

01:00:21.110 --> 01:00:26.670
is called Flow Group Reward
Policy Optimization a.k.a.

01:00:26.670 --> 01:00:28.870
Flow-GRPO.

01:00:28.870 --> 01:00:31.730
So for those of you who
are also in the LLM field,

01:00:31.730 --> 01:00:35.350
you may have heard
the term GRPO a lot

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:38.270
during the past year or so.

01:00:38.270 --> 01:00:41.310
And so here, the
idea is to leverage

01:00:41.310 --> 01:00:45.550
that algorithm in the
case of image generation.

01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:50.670
So in a very similar
fashion, what you do is you

01:00:50.670 --> 01:00:54.790
take a look at images that
are generated by your image

01:00:54.790 --> 01:01:00.310
generation model, and in
this case, what you want

01:01:00.310 --> 01:01:07.430
is for a given prompt to have a
diverse set of generated images.

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:09.480
So it's going a little
bit into the details,

01:01:09.480 --> 01:01:15.620
but the authors are
framing this via an SDE

01:01:15.620 --> 01:01:21.460
that increases the diversity
of images that are generated,

01:01:21.460 --> 01:01:25.780
so this is all so
that we can have,

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:29.980
for a given prompt,
a diverse set

01:01:29.980 --> 01:01:32.660
of images that are generated.

01:01:32.660 --> 01:01:37.200
And from there, compute
relative rewards,

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:41.980
meaning you pass
each prompt along

01:01:41.980 --> 01:01:47.140
with each image to some
black-box reward model,

01:01:47.140 --> 01:01:51.060
and it tells you whether or
not that is a good image.

01:01:51.060 --> 01:01:56.100
And what you do is you
compute a relative measure

01:01:56.100 --> 01:01:59.900
of how good that image was
with respect to the other image

01:01:59.900 --> 01:02:01.620
in your group.

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:03.820
So that's why this
method is called

01:02:03.820 --> 01:02:05.700
group Reward Policy
Optimization,

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:09.170
is because you
actually compute--

01:02:09.170 --> 01:02:11.230
so an advantage, that's
the technical term,

01:02:11.230 --> 01:02:17.570
which is the reward relative
to the reward in your group.

01:02:17.570 --> 01:02:21.690
And then based on that,
you update the policy which

01:02:21.690 --> 01:02:24.050
is your image generation model.

01:02:24.050 --> 01:02:26.270
And so one thing
that I want to say,

01:02:26.270 --> 01:02:30.410
and it's true for all of
these reward-based approaches,

01:02:30.410 --> 01:02:34.890
is your reward is not
necessarily something that

01:02:34.890 --> 01:02:38.810
is fully aligned with
what you care about.

01:02:38.810 --> 01:02:43.010
And so you can have a problem
of reward hacking, where

01:02:43.010 --> 01:02:48.250
your model is being
updated too much in a way

01:02:48.250 --> 01:02:50.690
to optimize the reward
that is not perfectly

01:02:50.690 --> 01:02:52.610
aligned with what you want.

01:02:52.610 --> 01:02:57.770
And so in order to have a
way to mitigate that issue,

01:02:57.770 --> 01:03:01.597
you typically have an
extra term in your loss.

01:03:01.597 --> 01:03:03.430
And so here in the slide
it's kind of small,

01:03:03.430 --> 01:03:09.230
but it's a KL divergence term
between the policy that you're

01:03:09.230 --> 01:03:12.710
training on and the old policy.

01:03:12.710 --> 01:03:14.310
So it's just a
way for you to not

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:20.530
have iterations, updates that
are too far from the old model.

01:03:23.510 --> 01:03:26.790
And the last one I will
mention, and here I'm

01:03:26.790 --> 01:03:29.510
sorry for this big
formulas, but I

01:03:29.510 --> 01:03:32.150
couldn't find a good
illustration for this one.

01:03:32.150 --> 01:03:35.510
So this one is
called Diffusion DPO.

01:03:35.510 --> 01:03:39.150
And Diffusion DPO is
the diffusion equivalent

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:45.110
of DPO, which is a supervised
method used in the LLM world

01:03:45.110 --> 01:03:47.830
to tune the weights
of your model

01:03:47.830 --> 01:03:50.550
with respect to
human preferences.

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:54.550
And here, what you need to take
away from these three lines

01:03:54.550 --> 01:04:00.950
of equations is that you want
to incentivize your model

01:04:00.950 --> 01:04:07.540
to predict cities associated
with "winning" images.

01:04:07.540 --> 01:04:10.000
You want to
incentivize your model,

01:04:10.000 --> 01:04:12.740
doing a better job
at predicting those,

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:18.340
and you want to incentivize
your model to do a worse job

01:04:18.340 --> 01:04:24.620
at predicting velocities
of the "bad" images.

01:04:24.620 --> 01:04:27.260
So if you do that,
you're going to have

01:04:27.260 --> 01:04:31.020
a higher chance of having the
winning images be generated

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:36.220
and a lower chance of having
your model generate the worse

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:40.180
images from being generated.

01:04:40.180 --> 01:04:41.320
Does this part make sense?

01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:43.240
So we have these three
lines of equations.

01:04:43.240 --> 01:04:45.520
You can stare at
it after the class.

01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:47.780
It's something
that also tells you

01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:52.060
that we want the model to be
better at predicting velocities

01:04:52.060 --> 01:04:55.380
of winning images, and
worse at predicting

01:04:55.380 --> 01:04:59.860
loss velocity of losing images.

01:04:59.860 --> 01:05:04.370
So yeah, so this is what this
loss is telling you to do.

01:05:04.370 --> 01:05:05.430
OK, great.

01:05:05.430 --> 01:05:10.330
So there is one last
thing I want to tell you.

01:05:10.330 --> 01:05:14.090
We want to make
our images better.

01:05:14.090 --> 01:05:18.050
And I want you to
tell me, or I'm

01:05:18.050 --> 01:05:23.730
asking if you would know
what kind of prompts

01:05:23.730 --> 01:05:27.470
would lead to that kind of
image at this point in time,

01:05:27.470 --> 01:05:29.810
in the training, if
you were to guess.

01:05:29.810 --> 01:05:33.570
So here on the left, you
have a beautiful image

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:35.590
of a Teddy bear reading a book.

01:05:35.590 --> 01:05:40.570
So the question here is what
prompt do you want to put?

01:05:40.570 --> 01:05:41.790
Anyone wants to try?

01:05:44.690 --> 01:05:45.750
Can be creative.

01:05:52.450 --> 01:05:55.250
You think it's a trick question.

01:05:55.250 --> 01:05:58.670
So I'm going to just
for the sake of time,

01:05:58.670 --> 01:06:00.870
tell you what the prompt was.

01:06:00.870 --> 01:06:03.830
So it's actually a
truncated prompt,

01:06:03.830 --> 01:06:06.050
and I'll just read
the first few words.

01:06:06.050 --> 01:06:09.150
So "A warm, intimate indoor
reading scene featuring a plush

01:06:09.150 --> 01:06:12.390
teddy bear as a central
object, and it talks about how

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:14.690
the objects are, how
the lighting is."

01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:19.670
And it's actually maybe four
times what is on the slide.

01:06:19.670 --> 01:06:25.430
So the point here is that
these alignment methods

01:06:25.430 --> 01:06:29.630
allow us to have much
greater control on what we

01:06:29.630 --> 01:06:31.950
want the output image to be.

01:06:31.950 --> 01:06:34.750
And there is,
therefore, a mismatch

01:06:34.750 --> 01:06:38.990
between what we teach
the model and what

01:06:38.990 --> 01:06:41.250
users tell to the model.

01:06:41.250 --> 01:06:44.350
Because let's be
honest, you and I,

01:06:44.350 --> 01:06:47.530
if we want to create an
image of a teddy bear,

01:06:47.530 --> 01:06:50.110
we'll just say "a teddy
bear reading a book."

01:06:50.110 --> 01:06:52.670
But we want something
that looks like this.

01:06:52.670 --> 01:06:55.750
So there needs to be
something in between that

01:06:55.750 --> 01:06:59.420
is able to prompt our
model well in a way that

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:02.220
will generate beautiful images.

01:07:02.220 --> 01:07:08.100
And there is a method called
prompts enhancement, which

01:07:08.100 --> 01:07:13.020
will take a given user
input and transform it

01:07:13.020 --> 01:07:18.300
into a very elegant,
long, detailed prompt that

01:07:18.300 --> 01:07:20.900
would be similar to
the kinds of prompts

01:07:20.900 --> 01:07:24.060
that the model has
been trained on.

01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:27.540
And so this is all for us to
have a beautiful image based

01:07:27.540 --> 01:07:31.580
on a prompt that is in
distribution, if you want,

01:07:31.580 --> 01:07:35.780
and that is leveraging the
capabilities of the model.

01:07:35.780 --> 01:07:39.140
So I just want to
take a pause here.

01:07:39.140 --> 01:07:41.560
So I know I'm doing a lot
of metaphors comparisons,

01:07:41.560 --> 01:07:47.100
but let's try to recap all
the steps we've been through

01:07:47.100 --> 01:07:49.740
by taking the following analogy.

01:07:49.740 --> 01:07:57.480
So let's assume that our
image generation model is us,

01:07:57.480 --> 01:08:01.320
is similar to us actually
learning how to cook.

01:08:01.320 --> 01:08:05.380
So pre-training is about
learning how to generate images.

01:08:05.380 --> 01:08:09.400
So here just learn about the
different kinds of foods.

01:08:09.400 --> 01:08:14.200
Post-training is about learning
how to generate "good" images.

01:08:14.200 --> 01:08:15.980
So here, what does good mean?

01:08:15.980 --> 01:08:18.800
So these are examples
that we give you,

01:08:18.800 --> 01:08:21.819
so supervised fine tuning
and continued training.

01:08:21.819 --> 01:08:24.120
Continued training
is new knowledge.

01:08:24.120 --> 01:08:29.520
So here, it's as if I was
reading about recipes.

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:33.160
So I read about recipes
of how to make good food.

01:08:33.160 --> 01:08:36.020
And then supervised fine-tuning
is about the aesthetics,

01:08:36.020 --> 01:08:38.740
it's about the prompts,
the instruction following.

01:08:38.740 --> 01:08:42.220
And so here, I'm just making
sure that the food looks great.

01:08:42.220 --> 01:08:44.285
The delivery is good.

01:08:44.285 --> 01:08:46.160
And then you have the
preference tuning stage

01:08:46.160 --> 01:08:49.680
where you want to make sure
to do more of the things

01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:52.120
that you may like
rather than not.

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:54.160
And in this food
analogy case, you

01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:57.390
have an inspector, food
inspector that comes,

01:08:57.390 --> 01:09:00.870
and you cook several
times the same dish.

01:09:00.870 --> 01:09:06.029
And inspector just
tastes how good

01:09:06.029 --> 01:09:08.229
each of the dishes
that you prepared

01:09:08.229 --> 01:09:10.490
was and it just tells
you which one was better.

01:09:10.490 --> 01:09:15.670
And you update your way
of cooking based on that.

01:09:15.670 --> 01:09:21.990
And this prompt enhancement
thing is the same as you being

01:09:21.990 --> 01:09:28.350
in a restaurant and a customer
walking in and telling you,

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:30.830
"I want meat."

01:09:30.830 --> 01:09:34.750
But for you, you just know
medium-rare kind of meat

01:09:34.750 --> 01:09:36.210
with a pinch of salt and so on.

01:09:36.210 --> 01:09:40.990
So you will have a waiter
between the customer

01:09:40.990 --> 01:09:48.270
and yourself that will
rephrase the customer's request

01:09:48.270 --> 01:09:51.430
in a way that aligns with
what you have learned.

01:09:51.430 --> 01:09:56.500
And this is exactly what
prompts enhancement is.

01:09:56.500 --> 01:09:58.380
I hope this made sense.

01:09:58.380 --> 01:10:02.580
And with that, let's go to the
third stage of the training

01:10:02.580 --> 01:10:05.460
lifecycle, which is tuning.

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:11.860
So as I mentioned, tuning is
about personalizing your model

01:10:11.860 --> 01:10:15.740
for a special use case
that you care about.

01:10:15.740 --> 01:10:20.340
And so here, the method that
I want to talk to you about

01:10:20.340 --> 01:10:23.260
is a method that you may
have seen on the internet

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:25.700
because a lot of people
have been using it.

01:10:25.700 --> 01:10:28.500
It's called DreamBooth.

01:10:28.500 --> 01:10:37.040
And the idea is that you
have something of interest,

01:10:37.040 --> 01:10:39.780
let's say a specific
teddy bear, that you

01:10:39.780 --> 01:10:44.540
want to have in the
images that you generate.

01:10:44.540 --> 01:10:46.780
You don't want any teddy bear.

01:10:46.780 --> 01:10:49.500
You want that
specific teddy bear.

01:10:49.500 --> 01:10:53.680
So what you want is to
personalize your model

01:10:53.680 --> 01:10:59.320
to generate images of that
very specific teddy bear.

01:10:59.320 --> 01:11:01.720
So here's what you would do.

01:11:01.720 --> 01:11:06.240
What you would do
is taking your model

01:11:06.240 --> 01:11:09.640
and then having a few images
of that very specific teddy

01:11:09.640 --> 01:11:13.800
bear that you have, take
images of that teddy bear

01:11:13.800 --> 01:11:17.560
with different poses,
and train your model

01:11:17.560 --> 01:11:23.000
to generate that teddy bear
based on a prompt that relies

01:11:23.000 --> 01:11:30.760
on a rare token, that is here
bracket V. That will teach

01:11:30.760 --> 01:11:37.240
your model to associate
that specific person/object

01:11:37.240 --> 01:11:41.720
reference with that token.

01:11:41.720 --> 01:11:44.040
So you do that.

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:47.080
So let's say you do that.

01:11:47.080 --> 01:11:52.170
Do you see an example or
something that can go wrong.

01:11:52.170 --> 01:11:55.990
If you train your model to
generate these images based

01:11:55.990 --> 01:11:58.600
on that prompt?

01:11:58.600 --> 01:12:00.890
OK, let's assume
you just do that.

01:12:05.870 --> 01:12:08.590
Yep.

01:12:08.590 --> 01:12:11.550
So the answer is it's
not diverse enough.

01:12:11.550 --> 01:12:14.590
So I think you're very,
very-- you're touching exactly

01:12:14.590 --> 01:12:18.750
the problem that we have at
hand, which is that you will

01:12:18.750 --> 01:12:21.630
specialize your model
too much into generating

01:12:21.630 --> 01:12:25.330
that and actually forgetting
the kinds of concepts,

01:12:25.330 --> 01:12:28.870
the kinds of images it was
able to produce before.

01:12:28.870 --> 01:12:31.390
And so for that reason,
so it's a great answer

01:12:31.390 --> 01:12:35.430
for that reason, what you
want is to also make sure

01:12:35.430 --> 01:12:39.950
that your model does not lose
the ability that it had before.

01:12:39.950 --> 01:12:44.310
And so that's why what you
would want to do is to have some

01:12:44.310 --> 01:12:49.980
reference of text-to-image pairs
that it was able to generate

01:12:49.980 --> 01:12:53.100
successfully, that you would
also tell it to generate

01:12:53.100 --> 01:12:59.680
successfully and combine the
two into a loss that tells you,

01:12:59.680 --> 01:13:05.100
"I want to generate images
with that specific teddy bear,

01:13:05.100 --> 01:13:08.140
but also preserving the
performance that I had

01:13:08.140 --> 01:13:10.540
in the past."

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:13.440
And this is called the
prior preservation loss.

01:13:13.440 --> 01:13:15.940
That's second term.

01:13:15.940 --> 01:13:18.420
So when you do that training--

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:19.220
and so yeah.

01:13:19.220 --> 01:13:20.900
So you prevent overfitting.

01:13:20.900 --> 01:13:23.740
So when you do that training,
what you're able to do

01:13:23.740 --> 01:13:27.900
is to use that rare special
token that you have trained

01:13:27.900 --> 01:13:34.880
your model on and then generate
new images in specific settings.

01:13:34.880 --> 01:13:39.640
So here the special token
teddy bear attending CME 296,

01:13:39.640 --> 01:13:43.420
so this is how teddy
bear would look like.

01:13:43.420 --> 01:13:46.360
So that method is great,
and what we're doing

01:13:46.360 --> 01:13:50.560
is updating our model and
in particular the model

01:13:50.560 --> 01:13:56.760
weights in order to reflect
what we want to generate.

01:13:56.760 --> 01:14:00.840
Well, the problem is your
model can be quite big.

01:14:00.840 --> 01:14:05.160
It can be as big as
billions of parameters.

01:14:05.160 --> 01:14:08.440
I believe the biggest
text-to-image models

01:14:08.440 --> 01:14:11.280
that are out there are in the
order of tens of billions,

01:14:11.280 --> 01:14:12.960
the open-source ones.

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:19.720
And so you would not want to
update all the weights, which

01:14:19.720 --> 01:14:22.600
is the reason why
there is a method that

01:14:22.600 --> 01:14:27.360
is very commonly used in the LLM
world, which is also something

01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:32.200
that people use in this
instance, which is called LoRA,

01:14:32.200 --> 01:14:36.320
and stands for
Low-Rank Adaptation.

01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:39.280
And what this method
does is instead

01:14:39.280 --> 01:14:43.360
of training all the
weights of your model, what

01:14:43.360 --> 01:14:50.110
it does is it only trains
a fraction by considering

01:14:50.110 --> 01:14:53.790
that the weights of your model
are the weights of the base

01:14:53.790 --> 01:14:58.950
model, plus two
low-rank matrices.

01:14:58.950 --> 01:15:01.310
And the reason why it's
called low-rank adaptation

01:15:01.310 --> 01:15:05.910
is because it leads to A
times B, that is low rank.

01:15:05.910 --> 01:15:07.990
And so when you do
that, it turns out

01:15:07.990 --> 01:15:11.110
that you can preserve
most of the performance

01:15:11.110 --> 01:15:14.950
but train that with a
fraction of the cost.

01:15:14.950 --> 01:15:19.990
So you will see methods that
are called "DreamBooth + LoRA."

01:15:19.990 --> 01:15:22.950
And that is what it is about.

01:15:22.950 --> 01:15:26.880
And I want to talk
about the pros

01:15:26.880 --> 01:15:28.130
and the cons of this approach.

01:15:28.130 --> 01:15:30.510
So the pro is--

01:15:30.510 --> 01:15:32.710
the good thing is if
you want to generate

01:15:32.710 --> 01:15:36.730
thousands and thousands of
images of your teddy bear, well,

01:15:36.730 --> 01:15:38.350
you can do that.

01:15:38.350 --> 01:15:41.290
And the results are
pretty high fidelity.

01:15:41.290 --> 01:15:43.170
They match pretty
well the details

01:15:43.170 --> 01:15:46.210
that are in the input images.

01:15:46.210 --> 01:15:49.370
But the con here
is if you need--

01:15:49.370 --> 01:15:54.050
or if you need to do that
for a lot of special cases,

01:15:54.050 --> 01:15:59.170
well, you need to absorb the
training costs, which is pretty

01:15:59.170 --> 01:16:01.570
time consuming and costly.

01:16:01.570 --> 01:16:05.690
And also, it's something
that you cannot really reuse

01:16:05.690 --> 01:16:10.970
if, for instance, you want
something else in your images.

01:16:10.970 --> 01:16:15.210
And I would just conclude by
saying that this approach is

01:16:15.210 --> 01:16:18.530
great if you're in
a use case where

01:16:18.530 --> 01:16:22.890
you need to generate many,
many, many, many images of one

01:16:22.890 --> 01:16:31.170
specific subject, or
person, or object.

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:36.210
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervin.

01:16:36.210 --> 01:16:37.850
Thank you, Afshin.

01:16:37.850 --> 01:16:39.730
So we're going to
continue together

01:16:39.730 --> 01:16:42.600
on that note of
efficiency and looking

01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:45.520
at distillation
techniques which aim

01:16:45.520 --> 01:16:49.040
at preserving some
of the high quality

01:16:49.040 --> 01:16:51.780
that we were able to
develop with these models,

01:16:51.780 --> 01:16:54.880
but at a lower cost.

01:16:54.880 --> 01:17:00.500
So all of these methods produce
state-of-the-art images,

01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:04.160
but in real life, you might be a
user that uses a service that is

01:17:04.160 --> 01:17:05.360
high volume.

01:17:05.360 --> 01:17:08.080
So the provider doesn't
have an incentive

01:17:08.080 --> 01:17:11.280
to spend a lot of
money on each query.

01:17:11.280 --> 01:17:15.520
So you don't want typically to
have 1,000 steps to generate

01:17:15.520 --> 01:17:17.040
a single image.

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:19.840
Similarly, if you're
in a business setting,

01:17:19.840 --> 01:17:22.720
you want to achieve
a given goal,

01:17:22.720 --> 01:17:25.480
and your goal is not
necessarily to be

01:17:25.480 --> 01:17:28.120
the best, but just good enough.

01:17:28.120 --> 01:17:31.160
And then that
trade-off between good

01:17:31.160 --> 01:17:33.200
in terms of quality
and cost is something

01:17:33.200 --> 01:17:36.200
that you're going
to be interested in.

01:17:36.200 --> 01:17:38.870
And then you can cite
other applications

01:17:38.870 --> 01:17:42.190
where real-time
properties are important.

01:17:42.190 --> 01:17:45.150
So this is why it's
worth investigating

01:17:45.150 --> 01:17:48.310
techniques that are fast.

01:17:48.310 --> 01:17:52.750
And then all of these are, of
course, linked to properties

01:17:52.750 --> 01:17:54.650
that we care about
as everyday users.

01:17:54.650 --> 01:17:56.910
So we don't want to
spend a lot, and we

01:17:56.910 --> 01:18:01.390
don't want to spend
resources inefficiently.

01:18:01.390 --> 01:18:06.750
And if I was to tell
you about distillation

01:18:06.750 --> 01:18:11.510
in the traditional world--

01:18:11.510 --> 01:18:15.290
so you would typically see
something that looks like this.

01:18:15.290 --> 01:18:19.270
You have a teacher
model that is big

01:18:19.270 --> 01:18:23.590
and a student model that is
typically of a smaller size.

01:18:23.590 --> 01:18:27.110
And you both want to
predict the same outputs.

01:18:27.110 --> 01:18:32.550
And here, for example, so
I'm borrowing these slides

01:18:32.550 --> 01:18:36.350
from CME 295, where
we wanted to distill

01:18:36.350 --> 01:18:39.090
some of the knowledge on
next token prediction.

01:18:39.090 --> 01:18:42.810
So what is important in
this distillation technique

01:18:42.810 --> 01:18:47.250
is truly the shape of the
distribution that is learned.

01:18:47.250 --> 01:18:52.170
And you can see that you can
get away with smaller models,

01:18:52.170 --> 01:18:54.810
directly anchoring
on it and then

01:18:54.810 --> 01:18:58.870
producing a level of quality
that is almost just as high.

01:18:58.870 --> 01:19:01.650
So just to give a
quantitative example,

01:19:01.650 --> 01:19:05.050
you may think of a
BERT and DistilBERT,

01:19:05.050 --> 01:19:11.530
where you save
quite a bit, I think

01:19:11.530 --> 01:19:14.450
60%, half of the
parameters, roughly,

01:19:14.450 --> 01:19:17.283
and you retain 97%
of the performance.

01:19:17.283 --> 01:19:19.450
So this is something that
we are interested in doing

01:19:19.450 --> 01:19:20.810
as well here.

01:19:20.810 --> 01:19:22.790
So in the LLM
world, for example,

01:19:22.790 --> 01:19:26.490
you would do so with the KL
divergence between the two.

01:19:26.490 --> 01:19:30.250
So here, the loss
says if we assume

01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:33.890
that the teacher
distribution is a true one,

01:19:33.890 --> 01:19:38.060
how bad is it to approximate
it with a student one?

01:19:38.060 --> 01:19:41.440
And this is how you perform
your loss optimization process

01:19:41.440 --> 01:19:43.800
with that kind of mindset.

01:19:43.800 --> 01:19:46.840
But here in the image
world, well, you

01:19:46.840 --> 01:19:51.460
can try to reduce the
size of the student model.

01:19:51.460 --> 01:19:55.200
And it typically leads
to much worse quality.

01:19:55.200 --> 01:19:57.960
And then you have
another knob that you

01:19:57.960 --> 01:20:02.040
have as a low-hanging fruit
that people typically tune,

01:20:02.040 --> 01:20:07.320
which is not the model size
but more the number of steps.

01:20:07.320 --> 01:20:12.660
So you might remember from the
first lecture that, for example,

01:20:12.660 --> 01:20:15.880
in diffusion you have,
let's say 1,000 steps.

01:20:15.880 --> 01:20:19.460
Similarly, in the flow matching
setting that afshin mentioned,

01:20:19.460 --> 01:20:24.320
we were considering
here, you typically

01:20:24.320 --> 01:20:25.400
have more than one step.

01:20:25.400 --> 01:20:27.400
And you would want
to make it one,

01:20:27.400 --> 01:20:32.080
so that just a single pass
gives you what you want.

01:20:32.080 --> 01:20:35.390
So here, we were not going
to be doing typically

01:20:35.390 --> 01:20:38.590
KL divergence between
distribution over tokens,

01:20:38.590 --> 01:20:41.710
but these kind of
loss that regresses

01:20:41.710 --> 01:20:44.870
the output with respect
to the teacher output

01:20:44.870 --> 01:20:46.730
is something that
you are going to see.

01:20:46.730 --> 01:20:50.330
So one way of doing it is
with the mean squared error.

01:20:50.330 --> 01:20:54.190
We're going to see other
techniques as well.

01:20:54.190 --> 01:20:58.790
And I just want us
to do this thought

01:20:58.790 --> 01:21:04.950
exercise of approximating in
one step directly the teacher

01:21:04.950 --> 01:21:07.270
output.

01:21:07.270 --> 01:21:12.990
So the issue is that it's
too hard because you can--

01:21:12.990 --> 01:21:16.110
so just to take the analogy
that Afshin was taking

01:21:16.110 --> 01:21:19.310
last week regarding
the painter, it's

01:21:19.310 --> 01:21:25.550
as if you ask a painter to
paint a beautiful piece of art

01:21:25.550 --> 01:21:28.190
and then ask the
students to do exactly

01:21:28.190 --> 01:21:30.670
the same with a single stroke.

01:21:30.670 --> 01:21:35.450
So it's way too much information
packed for a single forward step

01:21:35.450 --> 01:21:37.770
to capture all at once.

01:21:37.770 --> 01:21:41.130
So you need a more granular
subsequence of steps

01:21:41.130 --> 01:21:42.850
intuitively.

01:21:42.850 --> 01:21:46.290
And this is what the spirit
of progressive distillation

01:21:46.290 --> 01:21:55.530
is, where we take steps and ask
the students to work on problems

01:21:55.530 --> 01:21:59.370
that are of approximate
constant difficulty,

01:21:59.370 --> 01:22:05.730
and we iterate until we have a
model that does what we want.

01:22:05.730 --> 01:22:09.010
So in other words,
the formulation

01:22:09.010 --> 01:22:12.490
here is that we want the number
of total steps to go from big T

01:22:12.490 --> 01:22:14.570
to 1.

01:22:14.570 --> 01:22:18.530
So here the method
is as follows,

01:22:18.530 --> 01:22:21.810
every time you try to
do what you were doing

01:22:21.810 --> 01:22:24.290
in half the number of steps.

01:22:24.290 --> 01:22:27.330
So I want you to make
a thought experiment

01:22:27.330 --> 01:22:30.040
for a second
between Afshin and I

01:22:30.040 --> 01:22:33.320
and not suppose
that we are twins,

01:22:33.320 --> 01:22:36.280
but instead, we
are a tuple of size

01:22:36.280 --> 01:22:42.840
n of identical brothers, more
precisely of size log2 of number

01:22:42.840 --> 01:22:44.120
of steps.

01:22:44.120 --> 01:22:46.600
And here progressive
distillation

01:22:46.600 --> 01:22:51.560
would be Afshin, for
example, being the master

01:22:51.560 --> 01:22:53.960
painter for the first step.

01:22:53.960 --> 01:23:00.480
And every two strokes I would
paint one stroke, and so on.

01:23:00.480 --> 01:23:02.960
And then once the
process finishes,

01:23:02.960 --> 01:23:05.840
I would be the master
painter, and then

01:23:05.840 --> 01:23:08.780
the next identical brother
would be the student.

01:23:08.780 --> 01:23:11.240
So every two strokes
that I paint,

01:23:11.240 --> 01:23:15.460
he would paint one stroke,
so that at the end,

01:23:15.460 --> 01:23:18.940
you reach what you
want in a single step.

01:23:21.480 --> 01:23:25.800
So just to give a visual
representation of what

01:23:25.800 --> 01:23:27.280
we have learned here--

01:23:27.280 --> 01:23:32.630
so you can see this
as secant lines

01:23:32.630 --> 01:23:37.110
that you draw in the
space at every halving

01:23:37.110 --> 01:23:40.790
of the number of steps,
which is a simple problem

01:23:40.790 --> 01:23:42.510
to take in isolation.

01:23:42.510 --> 01:23:44.710
And then when you
do that iteratively,

01:23:44.710 --> 01:23:48.190
you reach the end goal.

01:23:48.190 --> 01:23:51.950
So looking at the results
here, the good thing

01:23:51.950 --> 01:23:55.070
about this technique
is that you can select

01:23:55.070 --> 01:23:57.670
the number of
function evaluations

01:23:57.670 --> 01:24:00.990
that you're ready
to spend, and you

01:24:00.990 --> 01:24:06.330
have an association
with the end result.

01:24:06.330 --> 01:24:08.190
So of course, if you
spend more steps,

01:24:08.190 --> 01:24:09.690
you're going to
have better quality,

01:24:09.690 --> 01:24:11.990
but you have this
level of granularity.

01:24:11.990 --> 01:24:15.430
And you see that you typically
have better performance

01:24:15.430 --> 01:24:18.710
if you were to compare
an equal number of steps,

01:24:18.710 --> 01:24:22.990
for example, in a deterministic
technique like DDIM,

01:24:22.990 --> 01:24:27.110
like we had seen in the
first lecture, which is nice.

01:24:29.650 --> 01:24:32.930
But here, one thing
that we're doing

01:24:32.930 --> 01:24:36.290
is that no matter
how hard the path

01:24:36.290 --> 01:24:38.770
to getting to the
clean image is,

01:24:38.770 --> 01:24:41.250
we're just taking it as a given.

01:24:41.250 --> 01:24:45.110
We're not saying, OK, let's
to make the problem simpler.

01:24:45.110 --> 01:24:47.450
We're just taking the
problem as hard as it is

01:24:47.450 --> 01:24:50.010
and we're trying
to work around it.

01:24:50.010 --> 01:24:55.370
And if you recall the third
lecture on flow matching,

01:24:55.370 --> 01:24:57.890
there is something that
we wish for that we

01:24:57.890 --> 01:25:01.290
might be able to
realize, which is

01:25:01.290 --> 01:25:07.490
the reflow technique that
helps in straightening paths

01:25:07.490 --> 01:25:08.930
in the space.

01:25:08.930 --> 01:25:11.450
So maybe we could
make use of it.

01:25:11.450 --> 01:25:18.830
And this is what the paper
called "InstaFlow" does.

01:25:18.830 --> 01:25:22.490
So it follows up on this
idea of rectified flow

01:25:22.490 --> 01:25:25.640
that we had seen at
the third lecture.

01:25:25.640 --> 01:25:28.840
So it does apply this method.

01:25:28.840 --> 01:25:33.760
And then on top of it, it
adds a layer of distillation

01:25:33.760 --> 01:25:38.840
to further solidify
the fact that we

01:25:38.840 --> 01:25:42.400
can get to the results
in a single step.

01:25:42.400 --> 01:25:48.160
So just to give you a recap on
what reflow was, so you had--

01:25:48.160 --> 01:25:51.800
so let's say you sample
from the easy distribution

01:25:51.800 --> 01:25:55.380
and then you integrate your
ODE to get to the sample.

01:25:55.380 --> 01:25:58.680
Then you take these
pairs of input-outputs

01:25:58.680 --> 01:26:02.920
and then you fit a new model,
like a new model in it.

01:26:02.920 --> 01:26:05.960
And what you get is
straighter path, typically,

01:26:05.960 --> 01:26:08.680
which allows you
to take less Euler

01:26:08.680 --> 01:26:11.640
steps to get to the answer.

01:26:11.640 --> 01:26:16.720
But I have a question for you.

01:26:16.720 --> 01:26:20.060
Let's say the problem
is not totally straight,

01:26:20.060 --> 01:26:23.440
would you be inclined
to do more reflow steps?

01:26:31.020 --> 01:26:32.420
So it's a trick question.

01:26:32.420 --> 01:26:35.580
So the answer is if you
do more reflow steps,

01:26:35.580 --> 01:26:38.700
then you introduce
discretization errors

01:26:38.700 --> 01:26:39.680
in your pairs.

01:26:39.680 --> 01:26:41.580
So the answer is typically no.

01:26:41.580 --> 01:26:46.820
And this is why what is
pretty convenient to do

01:26:46.820 --> 01:26:49.740
is to give the task
of still integrating

01:26:49.740 --> 01:26:53.780
that ODE to some teacher model
and then have a student fit

01:26:53.780 --> 01:26:54.960
directly on that output.

01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:59.140
So this is what the
distillation step does.

01:26:59.140 --> 01:27:03.700
And then one interesting
fact is that we use--

01:27:03.700 --> 01:27:07.260
so this concept of
distance between what

01:27:07.260 --> 01:27:10.660
the teacher predicts and
what the student gets to.

01:27:10.660 --> 01:27:14.740
So you have this
concept of warm-up,

01:27:14.740 --> 01:27:18.500
of using a simple
distance metric like MSE.

01:27:18.500 --> 01:27:20.740
But we have seen in
the previous lecture

01:27:20.740 --> 01:27:25.010
that MSE is not great to give
a meaningful interpretation

01:27:25.010 --> 01:27:27.870
into whether the quality
of the image is good,

01:27:27.870 --> 01:27:31.290
because it focuses on the
specific value of each pixel

01:27:31.290 --> 01:27:34.650
instead of the meaning
of the feature,

01:27:34.650 --> 01:27:40.530
which is why this method
uses the second loss

01:27:40.530 --> 01:27:47.170
formulation called LPIPS,
Learned Perceptual Image Patch

01:27:47.170 --> 01:27:49.330
Similarity.

01:27:49.330 --> 01:27:54.490
And here, you make the image
pass through a pre-trained model

01:27:54.490 --> 01:27:56.570
with frozen weights,
and then you

01:27:56.570 --> 01:27:59.010
compare the L2 distance
between the feature

01:27:59.010 --> 01:28:01.510
maps instead over L layers.

01:28:04.690 --> 01:28:06.950
And this is what we just said.

01:28:06.950 --> 01:28:11.170
And then you see in practice,
that the first reflow step gives

01:28:11.170 --> 01:28:15.370
you most of the
straightening behavior,

01:28:15.370 --> 01:28:18.570
so you meaningfully-- you
do not have an incentive

01:28:18.570 --> 01:28:21.560
to do more of them.

01:28:21.560 --> 01:28:26.680
And in terms of results,
you see that you exceed

01:28:26.680 --> 01:28:29.960
the performance of whether--

01:28:29.960 --> 01:28:33.120
of the fact that you add this
additional distillation step

01:28:33.120 --> 01:28:37.200
is better than just doing a
reflow and having a single step.

01:28:37.200 --> 01:28:43.560
So it's the InstaFlow 0.9B
versus the line just above that

01:28:43.560 --> 01:28:45.120
shows that.

01:28:45.120 --> 01:28:49.440
And yeah.

01:28:49.440 --> 01:28:52.600
So one thing that is
tough with InstaFlow

01:28:52.600 --> 01:28:55.920
is that every time
you do a reflow,

01:28:55.920 --> 01:28:58.180
it's an expensive process.

01:28:58.180 --> 01:29:01.100
So you need to do some
pre work, which is--

01:29:01.100 --> 01:29:03.560
like the cost you
have to pay here.

01:29:03.560 --> 01:29:07.800
And we're wondering
if there is something

01:29:07.800 --> 01:29:13.400
we could do better when it comes
to the path that are drawn.

01:29:13.400 --> 01:29:16.440
Is there some information that
we could give to the student

01:29:16.440 --> 01:29:21.180
instead of making the problem
simpler from the first place?

01:29:21.180 --> 01:29:24.638
But before I move on
to the next mindset

01:29:24.638 --> 01:29:27.180
that I wanted to mention, I just
wanted to pause for a second

01:29:27.180 --> 01:29:28.980
and see if there
is any questions.

01:29:28.980 --> 01:29:30.340
Yep.

01:29:30.340 --> 01:29:33.300
The question is, Why wouldn't
you force straight path

01:29:33.300 --> 01:29:34.200
in the pre-training?

01:29:34.200 --> 01:29:39.860
You already do that, because you
select a random noise at random

01:29:39.860 --> 01:29:44.700
and then a target point, and
then you draw a straight line.

01:29:44.700 --> 01:29:47.540
But the thing is, these
pairings, they're not optimal.

01:29:47.540 --> 01:29:49.923
So when you sample randomly--

01:29:49.923 --> 01:29:52.340
and then the fact that you
don't have these straight lines

01:29:52.340 --> 01:29:55.780
is not an intended
design, but it's

01:29:55.780 --> 01:29:59.580
a consequence of this MSE loss.

01:29:59.580 --> 01:30:04.320
So the reflow procedure
is our way to remedy that,

01:30:04.320 --> 01:30:08.920
and there is no way to do that
as early as the first train.

01:30:12.620 --> 01:30:14.260
OK, awesome.

01:30:14.260 --> 01:30:17.970
So I'm slightly late--

01:30:17.970 --> 01:30:25.440
so I'm going to go through
this next idea of leveraging

01:30:25.440 --> 01:30:28.970
local properties
to train a better

01:30:28.970 --> 01:30:34.850
student with this notion of
consistency models, which

01:30:34.850 --> 01:30:38.890
has an interesting property
of looking at paths that

01:30:38.890 --> 01:30:43.130
are deterministic with
a mapping that maps

01:30:43.130 --> 01:30:48.050
every point of that path
to the end goal, such that

01:30:48.050 --> 01:30:50.650
no matter where you
are on that path,

01:30:50.650 --> 01:30:54.210
if you train your model
to predict the end image,

01:30:54.210 --> 01:30:56.010
then you can make
the jump as early

01:30:56.010 --> 01:31:03.330
as when you are in the noise
in the noise distribution.

01:31:03.330 --> 01:31:09.330
So just putting a picture
here with our teddy bears

01:31:09.330 --> 01:31:14.660
and the flow matching case,
if you have such a property,

01:31:14.660 --> 01:31:21.360
then every point on a given
green line would map to x1.

01:31:21.360 --> 01:31:24.700
And the way to do so is twofold.

01:31:24.700 --> 01:31:29.960
So you can either do it with
so-called consistency training,

01:31:29.960 --> 01:31:34.680
where you sample a
clean image and then you

01:31:34.680 --> 01:31:38.080
noise it at some level
t, and you noise it

01:31:38.080 --> 01:31:40.800
at level t plus delta t.

01:31:40.800 --> 01:31:44.120
And what you do is that you
pass it through your model

01:31:44.120 --> 01:31:48.400
and try to make them
match to the same value.

01:31:48.400 --> 01:31:49.900
So this is one way of doing so.

01:31:49.900 --> 01:31:52.040
There is a second
way that leverages

01:31:52.040 --> 01:31:56.320
this teacher-student
relationship, where denoising

01:31:56.320 --> 01:31:59.640
is not something that you
would artificially do,

01:31:59.640 --> 01:32:02.160
but you would ask
a teacher model

01:32:02.160 --> 01:32:05.680
to do denoising, which
reflects properties

01:32:05.680 --> 01:32:09.600
that it has learned
in the feature space,

01:32:09.600 --> 01:32:16.460
and then you ask a student
model to make predictions that

01:32:16.460 --> 01:32:17.820
are consistent to each other.

01:32:21.020 --> 01:32:24.640
So here, you might say,
"OK, very well, Sherwin.

01:32:24.640 --> 01:32:29.300
But if you only force these
representations to be consistent

01:32:29.300 --> 01:32:32.500
with each other, you could
well have a student that says,

01:32:32.500 --> 01:32:35.680
OK, don't worry, I
will always predict 0.

01:32:35.680 --> 01:32:37.580
That way, they're
always consistent,

01:32:37.580 --> 01:32:41.420
and maybe my loss will
not be looking very pretty

01:32:41.420 --> 01:32:45.260
at the boundaries,
but it's fine."

01:32:45.260 --> 01:32:51.180
Well, you have a way to prevent
that collapse with two tools.

01:32:51.180 --> 01:32:55.780
So one is this prediction
that you get at every step,

01:32:55.780 --> 01:33:01.420
is a parameterized function of
weights that we put as design.

01:33:01.420 --> 01:33:03.420
So we have it enforced.

01:33:03.420 --> 01:33:07.700
And then the second
ingredient in this recipe

01:33:07.700 --> 01:33:13.850
is to not backpropagate through
both students, where doing so

01:33:13.850 --> 01:33:16.250
could lead to
symmetrical weights

01:33:16.250 --> 01:33:19.330
updates that might
not be those that

01:33:19.330 --> 01:33:21.690
actually match a fixed target.

01:33:21.690 --> 01:33:26.290
So we do that by keeping
one of the students

01:33:26.290 --> 01:33:29.530
a slow-moving average and
the other one actually doing

01:33:29.530 --> 01:33:33.770
the consistency prediction task.

01:33:33.770 --> 01:33:38.510
And when you look at
what results it gets,

01:33:38.510 --> 01:33:40.130
it's quite competitive.

01:33:40.130 --> 01:33:45.090
And you can do so
in the latent space.

01:33:45.090 --> 01:33:46.730
It's called LCM.

01:33:46.730 --> 01:33:49.250
You might see in the literature.

01:33:49.250 --> 01:33:51.410
But all of these
methods, what they did

01:33:51.410 --> 01:33:55.210
is that they focused on
matching a given target.

01:33:55.210 --> 01:33:59.410
And one thing that you see
when you have this L2-style

01:33:59.410 --> 01:34:04.810
regression loss is that you
have a regression to the mean,

01:34:04.810 --> 01:34:10.280
where images don't look as
crisp as you would like.

01:34:10.280 --> 01:34:13.220
And GAN solves this.

01:34:13.220 --> 01:34:16.480
So let's see if there is
some adversarial objective

01:34:16.480 --> 01:34:19.580
that we can introduce
here to make it better.

01:34:26.360 --> 01:34:28.240
So before we even do that--

01:34:28.240 --> 01:34:30.660
so we were looking
at the sample level,

01:34:30.660 --> 01:34:34.320
maybe if we look at things from
a distribution perspective,

01:34:34.320 --> 01:34:38.600
we could look at where we could
go in the sample space that

01:34:38.600 --> 01:34:43.240
looks where we want to go rather
than a straight single point.

01:34:43.240 --> 01:34:49.600
And so the mindset here is
to give a piece of noise

01:34:49.600 --> 01:34:53.400
to both the teacher
and student model,

01:34:53.400 --> 01:34:56.320
and then based on
the student output,

01:34:56.320 --> 01:35:01.320
noise it again and ask the
teacher model, and then a model

01:35:01.320 --> 01:35:05.960
that looks like the student
to try to denoise it.

01:35:05.960 --> 01:35:12.540
And then use their results to
infer what pieces of advice

01:35:12.540 --> 01:35:15.840
the teacher and student models
would give, respectively,

01:35:15.840 --> 01:35:20.420
to give the clean image,
and use that signal as a way

01:35:20.420 --> 01:35:24.940
to inform what the student
prediction should be.

01:35:24.940 --> 01:35:28.860
So we're going to look at this
loss in details in a second.

01:35:28.860 --> 01:35:31.820
And you complement
that by ensuring

01:35:31.820 --> 01:35:35.500
that the student's
predictions match roughly what

01:35:35.500 --> 01:35:38.580
the teacher predictions do.

01:35:38.580 --> 01:35:45.540
And we see empirically that this
helps stabilize the training.

01:35:45.540 --> 01:35:48.120
So looking at this
distribution loss--

01:35:48.120 --> 01:35:51.220
so what you're doing in
practice is the KL divergence

01:35:51.220 --> 01:35:56.740
between the distribution of
the fake image with respect

01:35:56.740 --> 01:36:00.500
to the real image
based on a fake input.

01:36:00.500 --> 01:36:03.580
And when you take the
gradient of it with respect

01:36:03.580 --> 01:36:08.290
to the parameters of
the student model,

01:36:08.290 --> 01:36:12.970
you get this nice interpretation
of a difference of scores

01:36:12.970 --> 01:36:14.970
that you could see as a
difference of velocities

01:36:14.970 --> 01:36:18.490
in the field of flow matching.

01:36:18.490 --> 01:36:22.170
And these respectively
means that we

01:36:22.170 --> 01:36:25.290
want to do more of
what the teacher says

01:36:25.290 --> 01:36:30.810
we should do and less of what
the student says we should do.

01:36:30.810 --> 01:36:36.130
And we multiply-- we do a dot
product such that the parameter

01:36:36.130 --> 01:36:38.990
updates reflects that goal.

01:36:42.370 --> 01:36:44.530
OK, great.

01:36:44.530 --> 01:36:46.410
Yeah, I want to
pause for one second

01:36:46.410 --> 01:36:50.210
just to check if
that makes sense.

01:36:50.210 --> 01:36:56.430
Yeah, so here-- so the question
is, What's real and fake?

01:36:56.430 --> 01:37:00.690
So here the paper uses
standard denomination

01:37:00.690 --> 01:37:05.270
like used in GAN to
denote respectively

01:37:05.270 --> 01:37:06.670
teacher and student.

01:37:06.670 --> 01:37:09.550
So "real" would be
your true image,

01:37:09.550 --> 01:37:12.550
like what generates the true
distribution that you want

01:37:12.550 --> 01:37:13.050
to reach.

01:37:13.050 --> 01:37:16.390
And then "fake" is corresponding
to the distribution

01:37:16.390 --> 01:37:20.070
of the student that we want to
make it as close to the real

01:37:20.070 --> 01:37:21.910
ones.

01:37:21.910 --> 01:37:25.910
So respectively,
teacher and student.

01:37:25.910 --> 01:37:28.010
Yeah, great point.

01:37:28.010 --> 01:37:32.110
So the question, Is there
a collapse here happening?

01:37:32.110 --> 01:37:33.310
So it might.

01:37:33.310 --> 01:37:35.910
So if you were not doing that
comparison between teacher

01:37:35.910 --> 01:37:39.990
and student advices, you
would collapse to the mode

01:37:39.990 --> 01:37:42.370
of the teacher advice.

01:37:42.370 --> 01:37:46.070
And then the paper does
some ablation study on it.

01:37:46.070 --> 01:37:49.210
And then the second piece
that I mentioned earlier on,

01:37:49.210 --> 01:37:52.890
if you do not regress
with the teacher's output,

01:37:52.890 --> 01:37:56.190
you might see another--

01:37:56.190 --> 01:37:59.830
not really collapse, but you
would not reach the desired

01:37:59.830 --> 01:38:02.620
teacher distribution.

01:38:02.620 --> 01:38:08.380
So yeah, the answer
is the collapse

01:38:08.380 --> 01:38:10.760
is bad if you were not
considering this setup.

01:38:10.760 --> 01:38:12.900
But based on this
setup, I think there

01:38:12.900 --> 01:38:17.340
is some hyperparameter tuning
on how to balance the regression

01:38:17.340 --> 01:38:19.300
and distribution loss.

01:38:19.300 --> 01:38:21.660
Yep.

01:38:21.660 --> 01:38:22.280
Awesome.

01:38:22.280 --> 01:38:28.700
So moving on-- so I'm going
to make a series of complaints

01:38:28.700 --> 01:38:29.760
that are very targeted.

01:38:29.760 --> 01:38:32.540
So you're going to
see where I'm going.

01:38:32.540 --> 01:38:35.860
So here we're comparing
things with respect

01:38:35.860 --> 01:38:41.593
to the teacher output, which
is not the ground truth.

01:38:41.593 --> 01:38:42.760
So what I've mentioned here.

01:38:42.760 --> 01:38:44.820
So I've hovered over it.

01:38:44.820 --> 01:38:46.980
But the teacher--
the student advice

01:38:46.980 --> 01:38:51.540
is actually a diffusion
loop that you're running,

01:38:51.540 --> 01:38:53.740
which is quite expensive.

01:38:53.740 --> 01:38:57.260
And it's a bit of a duplicate
concept with respect

01:38:57.260 --> 01:39:01.290
to the student model that you're
training in the first place.

01:39:01.290 --> 01:39:05.770
And this idea of providing
signal with respect

01:39:05.770 --> 01:39:10.310
to teacher minus student advice
might seem as a soft one.

01:39:10.310 --> 01:39:12.690
Is there a way to
reframe it better?

01:39:12.690 --> 01:39:17.950
And yes, with GAN-like loss.

01:39:17.950 --> 01:39:20.930
So this is what ADD does.

01:39:20.930 --> 01:39:25.250
And to be mindful
of your time, I just

01:39:25.250 --> 01:39:27.770
want to say the high-level
is that it reframes

01:39:27.770 --> 01:39:30.130
as a classification
problem that makes

01:39:30.130 --> 01:39:35.170
the problem harder in the sense
that it will lead the student

01:39:35.170 --> 01:39:38.490
model to make
predictions crisper.

01:39:38.490 --> 01:39:41.650
But the issue with
all of this is

01:39:41.650 --> 01:39:46.050
that you have a lot of
back-and-forth going

01:39:46.050 --> 01:39:48.610
from the pixel to
the latent space

01:39:48.610 --> 01:39:55.370
that later techniques, such
as LADD, tries to mitigate.

01:39:55.370 --> 01:39:58.030
And this is what you're
going to be used quite a bit

01:39:58.030 --> 01:40:03.710
today, so you can reformulate
that only in latent space.

01:40:03.710 --> 01:40:07.710
And just to give some
final words here--

01:40:07.710 --> 01:40:09.710
so I want to say that
all of these distillation

01:40:09.710 --> 01:40:12.270
techniques-- they offer
some kind of trade-off.

01:40:12.270 --> 01:40:14.950
For example,
consistency models--

01:40:14.950 --> 01:40:16.910
they might be very
easily tunable

01:40:16.910 --> 01:40:20.350
with sets of LoRA weights,
as Afshin mentioned,

01:40:20.350 --> 01:40:23.170
whereas, for example if
you go into GAN territory,

01:40:23.170 --> 01:40:25.270
you have some dynamics
in the training process

01:40:25.270 --> 01:40:27.710
that are hard to manage.

01:40:27.710 --> 01:40:34.950
And yet this duality between MSE
and LPIPS loss for regression

01:40:34.950 --> 01:40:38.510
happens quite a bit, and
switching the mindset

01:40:38.510 --> 01:40:43.310
to a distribution and
GAN-based is great for quality.

01:40:43.310 --> 01:40:44.490
And yeah.

01:40:44.490 --> 01:40:46.270
And then trends are
these days to do

01:40:46.270 --> 01:40:48.710
all of this in latent space.

01:40:48.710 --> 01:40:50.430
And that's all I had for you.

01:40:50.430 --> 01:40:52.780
Thanks, and have
a great weekend.

